    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Taxation",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6411\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act in relation to approving a certain agreement\nfor a payment in lieu of taxes\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nRelates to eligibility of a Qualified Empire Zone Enterprise for real\nproperty tax credit for payments in lieu of taxes previously made for\na specified property.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one relates to eligibility for real property tax credits for\npayments in lieu of taxes made by a Qualified Empire Zone Enterprise\nfor property located at 8 Empire Drive, East Greenbush, New York.\n\nSection two allows a claim for a credit or refund for the property for\nspecific tax years.\n\nSection three is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nQualified Empire Zone Enterprises (QEZE) are vital businesses within\nour communities that encourage job growth and foster economic\ndevelopment. As an incentive to participate, these entities qualify\nfor certain tax exemptions and refunds. For example, under New York\nState tax law, a QEZE tenant that is responsible for property taxes\nunder its lease is eligible for a refund for paid property taxes.\nPayments by a lessee made pursuant to a PILOT agreement qualify as\nproperty taxes that are eligible for this refund.\n\nHowever, a policy shift within the New York State Department of\nTaxation and Finance resulted in the denial of credit for lessee\npayments as a result of the lessee not being a \"party to\" the PILOT\nagreement between the landlord and the tax jurisdiction. As a result,\nlessees who have been approved for the tax credit, have paid taxes\nconsistent with their lease agreements, and have been audited by the\nstate during the relevant time period, are now being assessed for\ncredits awarded in prior years or denied credits entirely.\n\nThese entities have complied with the intent and spirit of the law,\nand have relied on the tax credit as a significant component of their\neconomic development projects. Laws were enacted in 2012, 2013, 2014\nand 2016 to provide redress for certain affected QEZEs in different\nparts of the state. This bill would provide similar relief.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nSimilar to Chapters 404 and 435 of 2016, Chapters 350 and 444 of 2014,\nChapters 367 and 508 of 2013, and Chapter 430 of 2012.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone - such credits were previously accounted for in the State's\nfinancial plan.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to taxable\nyears beginning on or after January 1, 2006 and before January 1,\n2012.\n",
